    "body": "The most affordable Volkswagen ID. Polo Trend starts at just €24,995, or about $27,500, but you’ll have to wait close to two years to get one.\nVolkswagen ID. Polo orders lead to delivery delays\nThe new ID. Polo is “an affordable friend for everyday life,” as Volkswagen’s chief designer Andreas Mindt calls it.\nAfter opening orders for the ID. Polo with the 52 kWh battery at the end of April, starting at 33,795 euros ($37,000), Volkswagen made good on its promise, launching the lower-priced, smaller-battery model in July.\nThe VW ID. Polo Trend with the 37 kWh battery went on sale in Europe in July, starting at just 24,995 euros, or about $27,500, with a WLTP range of 207 miles (334 km).\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nBy mid-September, reports claimed Volkswagen’s entry-level EV was already essentially sold out across actross all trims, with more than 30,000 orders and a 10-month waitlist.\nAccording to the latest update from German automotive news outlet Edison Media , that number has doubled in about two months, and buyers now face delivery times of up to 21 months for the base ID. Polo Trend. Deliveries for the sporty GTI version are expected to be around seven months.\nVolkswagen ID. Polo (Source: Volkswagen)\n“We clearly planned too conservatively,” a VW manager told Edison during an interview. While VW expected about 90 to 95% to choose to lease, the leasing rate is only about 50%.\nThe larger 52 kWh battery version has also seen higher-than-expected demand, while 90% surprisingly opted to buy the optional €810 ($890) detachable towbar.\nAs a result, the company is prioritizing higher-margin models, which is likely leading to longer delivery times for the base ID. Polo.\nVolkswagen ID. Polo GTI (Source: Volkswagen)\nThe entry-level variants “just clog up to the line,” Volkswagen’s manager said during the recent interview.\nDemand for the ID. Cross, the electric equivalent to Volkswagen’s T-Cross, is also already exceeding expectations.\nVolkswagen ID. Polo interior (Source: Volkswagen)\nTop comment by MJE\nLiked by 2 people\nHow it could possibly take the makers of the Beetle and Golf (their two most successful products) to learn that small efficient relativity inexpensive cars would be popular (and their bloated ID4/5 things a bit of a flop) is hard to understand.\nOf course all these cars too expensive to succeed out side the protectionist European market. Maybe they will work out how to be globally price competitive one day. I live in hope.\nView all comments\nVolkswagen opened orders for the entry-level ID. Cross Trend last month, starting at €27,995 ($31,000) in Germany, including VAT. The 52 kWh battery version will go on sale in the UK this week, starting at £30,260 ($40,000). The ID. Cross Life with the 37 kWh battery will go on sale in January 2027, starting at £26,985 ($35,500).\nElectrek’s Take\nVolkswagen’s new entry-level electric cars are off to a promising start. The electric urban car family, which includes the ID. Polo, ID. Cross, and the CUPRA Raval and Skoda Epiq, has received nearly 150,000 orders combined since production began in June.\nHowever, the German automaker is still facing an uphill battle. The VW Group posted an operating loss of 5.9 billion euros ($6.5 billion) in the third quarter, its worst quarterly loss since the Dieselgate scandal.\nIn September, Volkswagen’s supervisory board approved its Future Plan 2030, which calls for reducing its global workforce by up to 100,000 , closing plants, and narrowing its vehicle lineup to cut costs.\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on X , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
